'''Information metabolism''' denotes Kępiński's theoretical account of psychic life in terms of exchanges with an environment, drawing analogies to living systems. Its historical formulations and later reconstructions need to be distinguished from empirical models that can be specified and tested independently.

== Intellectual provenance and competing reconstructions ==
A historical-critical account of Kępiński's work situates the theory in his psychiatric practice and research on the Auschwitz syndrome ([https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26488267/ historical review]). Kokoszka's 1999 account presents information metabolism by analogy to energy metabolism; the analogy relates information processing to the maintenance and organization of the experiencing subject ([https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10372645/ Kokoszka 1999]).

Ceklarz (2018) challenges the identification of the information-metabolism concept with a specific cell-structural biological model. This disagreement matters: refuting a literal physiological analogy need not refute a descriptive framework of person–environment relations, and accepting a descriptive framework does not validate a cellular implementation. Ceklarz (2021) further interprets emotion and bodily condition as implicated in choice and subjective significance. The proposed connection with later somatic-marker accounts is an interpretive comparison, not experimental demonstration that the models are identical.

== Distinct explanatory levels ==
{| class="wikitable"
! Level !! Possible object of investigation !! Evidentiary boundary
|-
| Physiology || Neural and bodily responses during sensory or affective tasks || Requires physiological measurements and an independently specified mechanism
|-
| Cognitive process || Selection, retention, interpretation and use of observations || Requires behavioral predictions and comparison with alternative models
|-
| Phenomenological/clinical || Subjective experience and relations with surroundings || Requires appropriate first-person and clinical methods; cannot be replaced by a signal statistic alone
|-
| Mathematical || Probability distributions and uncertainty in observations || Requires defined variables, sampling conditions and units
|}

The theory's use of entropy should not be interpreted as a quantitative equivalence between psychiatric distress, thermodynamic entropy and Shannon entropy. For a discrete variable X, <math>H(X)=-\sum_x p(x)\log_2p(x)</math> depends on an explicit probability distribution. Changing the encoding, observation alphabet or time window can change the measured entropy without demonstrating any change in subjective meaning. Physical entropy requires a specified physical state description and appropriate units.

== Operationalization as a research proposal ==
A contemporary study could isolate one hypothesized relationship, such as whether affective appraisal moderates the association between sensory uncertainty and decisions. In an illustrative operational model, let X denote stimulus condition, B a measured bodily or self-reported state, A the selected action and Y the outcome. A candidate conditional model <math>P(A\mid X,B)</math> can be compared with a stimulus-only model <math>P(A\mid X)</math> using held-out trials and calibration. A randomized manipulation of stimulus uncertainty, carefully chosen affect measure, and preregistered analysis would help distinguish a predictive association from a causal effect. State measurement, individual differences, task incentives and participant expectations are potential confounders. Such a study tests a restricted modern hypothesis; neither notation nor an improved prediction score validates the historical theory as a whole.

The same distinction applies to computational agents: inputs, internal state, actions and feedback can be defined and measured, but labeling a state “emotion” does not establish human-like affect or experience.

== Scope, applications and limitations ==
Information metabolism is useful for historical analysis of psychiatric theories and for generating questions about the relation between bodily states, meaning and adaptive behavior. Its conceptual breadth creates a risk of post hoc explanations unless predictions, competing models and exclusion criteria are specified. It does not supply an independently validated diagnostic instrument, a conservation law for meaningful information or a universal theory of consciousness. The later typological framework [[Socionics/Technical|socionics]] borrows terminology; its categorical and interpersonal predictions require separate validation.

